or AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will contract prices for AI-grade memory (DDR5 DRAM, HBM and NAND) rise in calendar 2026, as memory becomes a quarter or more of the cost of an AI rack?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The year-on-year change in contract prices for DDR5 DRAM, HBM and NAND in calendar 2026, as reported by memory-market price trackers and the memory makers' own price and bit-shipment disclosures.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2026 contract prices, settled by Q4 2026 contract-price releases and memory makers' Q4 2026 results (published around early 2027).\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research's bill-of-materials analysis estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation; the bank estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, and says the GPUs are not the driver of the rack price increase.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In Morgan Stanley's analysis, memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is put at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5 to 10 percent on GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"The Morgan Stanley analysis reported by MarketScale states that first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is the force behind the higher rack price and is now raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the roughly $7.8 million estimate, a reminder that procurement structure materially moves the per-rack number.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 79,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. The bank attributes the increase to components other than the GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to Morgan Stanley's analysis reported by MarketScale, first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin (VR200 NVL72) racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content in Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. On the bill of materials, memory rises to 25-30% on the VR200 from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, with constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand raising component costs across the market, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. Memory accounts for a quarter or more of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack value, so the same squeeze is an input-cost and availability factor for those racks.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the price of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack down to around $6.7 million, meaning procurement structure can move final rack pricing by more than $1 million per unit.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 80,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market.\",\n     \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-memory-prices\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will contract prices for the DRAM/LPDDR5X and NAND memory used in AI data-center racks rise in calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The calendar-2026 change in industry contract prices for DDR5/LPDDR5X DRAM and NAND flash, the memory content that now makes up roughly 25–30% of an NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack's bill of materials (about $2 million per rack).\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026, measured by the Q4 2026 industry contract price data (TrendForce/DRAMeXchange series); the item (published 2026-07-06) gives no release date, so the calendar-year contract price series' release fixes the check date.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; the GPUs are not the driver of the increase.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-memory-prices\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"In the Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials breakdown cited by the article, memory's share of the Vera Rubin VR200 rack bill of materials rose to 25–30% from 5–10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-memory-prices\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"First shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, per the article's account of Morgan Stanley's analysis.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus about $7.8 million, a reminder that procurement structure materially moves the per-rack number.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market: contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-memory-prices\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 81,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"E1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, with the GPUs not driving the increase; the bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"E2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a roughly threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5-10% on GB200 to 25-30% on VR200 while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"E3\",\n    \"summary\": \"First shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"E4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, reflecting the same constrained DRAM/NAND/HBM supply against AI-driven demand that is raising component costs tied to the AI buildout.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. The AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"memdiag-packet-claims-3.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps({\\\"n\\\":x[\\\"n\\\"],\\\"claims\\\":x[\\\"claims\\\"],\\\"link_rule\\\":x[\\\"link_rule\\\"],\\\"usable\\\":x[\\\"answer_usable\\\"],\\\"evidence\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":e[\\\"id\\\"],\\\"summary\\\":e[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"links\\\":e[\\\"links\\\"]} for e in x[\\\"kept_evidence\\\"]]})) for x in p]' \"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"memdiag-packet-claims-3.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps({\\\"n\\\":x[\\\"n\\\"],\\\"claims\\\":x[\\\"claims\\\"],\\\"link_rule\\\":x[\\\"link_rule\\\"],\\\"usable\\\":x[\\\"answer_usable\\\"],\\\"evidence\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":e[\\\"id\\\"],\\\"summary\\\":e[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"links\\\":e[\\\"links\\\"]} for e in x[\\\"kept_evidence\\\"]]})) for x in p]' \"","aggregated_output":"{\"n\": 55, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation; memory's share of the rack bill of materials rises to 25-30% on VR200 from 5-10% on GB200 while the GPU's share falls to roughly 51% from about 63%, and Morgan Stanley notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates that NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when the chips are sold in volume.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\", \"links\": [\"t1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, as reported in the article's account of the Morgan Stanley cost analysis.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": [\"t1\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 56, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. In the bank's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content is about $2 million per rack (a 435% jump from the prior generation), and memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5\\u201310% on GB200 to 25\\u201330% on VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\", \"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\", \"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev3\", \"summary\": \"First shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev4\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market: contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\", \"clean-4\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 57, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. It estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, with memory content at about $2 million per rack (up 435%), memory at 25-30% of the bill of materials versus 5-10% on GB200, and the GPU share falling from about 63% to 51%. Morgan Stanley also estimates that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\", \"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs, with contract DDR5 pricing climbing sharply; it says memory supply tightness and lead times for AI racks now deserve the same scheduling attention as accelerator allocation.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\", \"clean-6\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 58, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates in a bill-of-materials analysis that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, as reported by MarketScale.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage; memory's share of the bill of materials rose to 25\\u201330% from 5\\u201310% on GB200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%, and Morgan Stanley notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\", \"links\": [\"new-mem-price\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter; the passage follows the account of Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis and names no separate source for the schedule.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, driven by constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": [\"new-mem-price\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 59, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"A Morgan Stanley Research bill-of-materials analysis, reported by MarketScale, estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. Morgan Stanley estimates memory content at about $2 million per rack (a 435% jump), driven by a threefold rise in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage, and estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5-10% on the GB200 to 25-30% on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%. First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth. Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\", \"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. It notes that with memory representing a quarter or more of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack value and memory supply tight, memory availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 60, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, nearly double the roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and the bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; the bank notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Nvidia Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory's share of the rack bill of materials rises to 25-30% on the VR200 from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research's bill-of-materials analysis has first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products; it adds that memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 61, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e-1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, with NVIDIA charging about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; the bank adds that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, so procurement structure can move final pricing by $1 million or more per rack.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e-2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5\\u201310% on GB200 to 25\\u201330% on VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": [\"mt-1\", \"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e-3\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research reports that first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e-4\", \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is the force raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": [\"mt-1\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 62, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research's bill-of-materials analysis estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation \\u2014 the overall system price nearly doubling generation over generation \\u2014 with memory rising to 25\\u201330% of the bill of materials on the VR200 (from 5\\u201310% on the GB200) and the GPU's share falling from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a roughly threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, adding that the GPUs themselves are not the driver of the rack cost increase.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million from the bank's roughly $7.8 million estimate, saying procurement structure now materially moves the number.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"In Morgan Stanley's analysis, first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e6\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market: contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. It adds that, with memory supply tight, availability and lead times deserve the same scheduling attention as accelerator allocation.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 63, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and the bank estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; the increase is attributed to components other than the GPU.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5\\u201310 percent on the GB200 to 25\\u201330 percent on the VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's Vera Rubin systems are scheduled to make first shipments in the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, according to the article; the schedule is reported without naming who set it.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that contract DDR5 memory pricing has climbed sharply as constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory meets AI-driven demand, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev-5\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the price of an NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack down to around $6.7 million from roughly $8 million, indicating that procurement structure materially moves the per-rack number.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 64, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research's bill-of-materials analysis estimates NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. Memory content is put at about $2 million per rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage virtually absent from earlier systems. Memory's share of the bill of materials rises to 25-30% on the VR200 from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\", \"new-memory-2026\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, and says the GPUs themselves are not the driver of the rack price increase.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, according to the schedule reported in Morgan Stanley Research's bill-of-materials analysis.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that with memory representing a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, memory availability and lead times warrant the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\", \"links\": [\"new-memory-2026\", \"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, showing that procurement structure can move the final rack price by more than $1 million per rack.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 65, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, according to the bill-of-materials analysis cited by MarketScale.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\", \"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when those chips are sold in volume.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage; memory's share of the bill of materials rises from 5-10% on GB200 to 25-30% on VR200 while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"In the analysis cited by MarketScale, first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass the higher memory costs into upcoming products, tying the AI buildout to the same memory supply squeeze as consumer devices.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e6\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, meaning procurement structure can move final pricing by more than $1 million per rack.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 66, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. In the bank's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content is about $2 million per rack (a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a roughly threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage), memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5\\u201310% on the GB200 to 25\\u201330% on the VR200, and the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"According to the Morgan Stanley Research analysis reported by MarketScale, first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"summary\": \"The item reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is the force behind the higher Vera Rubin rack price and is raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules for NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the roughly $7.8 million estimated purchase price, indicating procurement structure materially moves the final number.\", \"links\": []}]}\n{\"n\": 67, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research's bill-of-materials analysis estimates Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. Memory content per rack is put at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, on a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage; as a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5\\u201310% on GB200 to 25\\u201330% on VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": [\"new-mem-price\", \"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research's breakdown has Nvidia's first Vera Rubin shipments scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is pushing up component costs, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": [\"new-mem-price\", \"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, and that procurement structure can move final pricing by $1+ million per rack.\", \"links\": [\"new-mem-price\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 68, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation; the bank estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU in volume, and notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content in NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million per rack, a 435% increase over the prior generation, driven by a threefold rise in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage; memory's share of the bill of materials rose to 25\\u201330% on VR200 from 5\\u201310% on GB200, while the GPU share fell from about 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\", \"new-memory-price\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"The item reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs market-wide, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that with memory representing a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, availability and lead times need the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\", \"new-memory-price\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 69, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research, reported by MarketScale, estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, with Nvidia charging about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. The bank puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND; memory's share of the bill of materials rises to 25-30% on the VR200 from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%, and a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\", \"memory_prices_2027\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that contract DDR5 memory pricing has climbed sharply, that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that the AI buildout and consumer-device memory costs are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\", \"links\": [\"memory_prices_2027\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 70, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. Memory is the driver of the increase: about $2 million of memory content per rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND. Memory rises from 5-10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25-30% on VR200, while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%. Reported by MarketScale from Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, with the article stating the GPUs themselves are not the driver of the rack price increase.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"summary\": \"The item reports that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter; no direct attribution for the schedule is given in the article.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, and the article states procurement structure can move final rack pricing by more than $1 million per rack.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-5\", \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": []}]}\n{\"n\": 71, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates, in a bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\", \"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"According to the Morgan Stanley Research analysis reported by MarketScale, first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin (VR200) racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter; the item gives no Nvidia confirmation of the schedule.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a roughly threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory's share of the bill of materials rises to 25\\u201330% on VR200 from 5\\u201310% on GB200, while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\", \"new-mem-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin VR200 rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus its roughly $7.8 million estimate, showing that procurement structure materially moves the per-rack figure.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-5\", \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports, citing constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand as the force behind rising AI rack prices, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": [\"new-mem-1\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 72, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, with NVIDIA charging about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory rose from 5 to 10 percent of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev3\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's analysis states NVIDIA's Vera Rubin first shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev4\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory is constrained against AI-driven demand, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, tying the AI buildout and laptop memory prices to the same supply squeeze.\", \"links\": []}]}\n{\"n\": 73, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-rack-price\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\", \"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-memory-bom\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; as a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5-10 percent on the GB200 to 25-30 percent on the VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\", \"links\": [\"new-memory-price\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-ship-schedule\", \"summary\": \"According to the Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter of 2026.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-memory-supply-price\", \"summary\": \"The item reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass the higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": [\"new-memory-price\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-self-sourcing\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the price of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack down to around $6.7 million, meaning procurement structure can move final rack pricing by more than $1 million per rack.\", \"links\": [\"new-memory-price\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 74, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"According to a Morgan Stanley Research bill-of-materials analysis cited by MarketScale, Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, and notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\", \"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's analysis puts memory content for Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million per rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage. Memory's share of the bill of materials rose to 25\\u201330% on the VR200 from 5\\u201310% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"The Morgan Stanley-cited bill-of-materials analysis states that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, with contract DDR5 pricing having climbed sharply and device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products; it says AI-buildout demand and laptop memory prices are tied to the same supply squeeze. It flags memory supply tightness and lead times as a scheduling risk for racks that deserves the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 75, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-rack-price-7-8m\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and the bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; Morgan Stanley says the GPUs themselves are not the driver of the generation-over-generation increase.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev-memory-bom-share\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis puts memory content per Nvidia Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory rose from 5-10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25-30% on VR200 while the GPU share fell from roughly 63% to 51%, and Morgan Stanley notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\", \"links\": [\"new-memory-contract-price-2027\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-memory-supply-price-squeeze\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products; it adds that with memory a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, memory availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\", \"links\": [\"new-memory-contract-price-2027\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-vera-rubin-first-shipments-q3-2026\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research's analysis states that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 76, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, with memory now accounting for 25-30% of total cost rather than 5-10%.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev3\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was largely absent before; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5-10% on the GB200 to 25-30% on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev4\", \"summary\": \"Per Morgan Stanley's analysis reported by MarketScale, first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev5\", \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, with contract DDR5 pricing having climbed sharply and device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev6\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, showing that procurement structure can materially move final pricing by over $1 million per rack.\", \"links\": []}]}\n{\"n\": 77, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-rack-cost\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; the bank adds that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-memory-bom\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; on that basis memory rises from 5 to 10 percent of the GB200 bill of materials to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\", \"links\": [\"memory-contract-price-2026\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-first-shipments\", \"summary\": \"According to Morgan Stanley's analysis, first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin (VR200 NVL72) racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-memory-supply\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is the force behind rising component costs, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": [\"memory-contract-price-2026\", \"clean-2\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 78, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research's bill-of-materials analysis estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation; the bank estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, and says the GPUs are not the driver of the rack price increase.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"In Morgan Stanley's analysis, memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is put at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5 to 10 percent on GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"The Morgan Stanley analysis reported by MarketScale states that first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is the force behind the higher rack price and is now raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the roughly $7.8 million estimate, a reminder that procurement structure materially moves the per-rack number.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 79, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. The bank attributes the increase to components other than the GPUs.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"According to Morgan Stanley's analysis reported by MarketScale, first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin (VR200 NVL72) racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content in Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. On the bill of materials, memory rises to 25-30% on the VR200 from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\", \"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, with constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand raising component costs across the market, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. Memory accounts for a quarter or more of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack value, so the same squeeze is an input-cost and availability factor for those racks.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-5\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the price of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack down to around $6.7 million, meaning procurement structure can move final rack pricing by more than $1 million per unit.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 80, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; the GPUs are not the driver of the increase.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\", \"links\": [\"new-memory-prices\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"In the Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials breakdown cited by the article, memory's share of the Vera Rubin VR200 rack bill of materials rose to 25\\u201330% from 5\\u201310% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": [\"new-memory-prices\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"First shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, per the article's account of Morgan Stanley's analysis.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus about $7.8 million, a reminder that procurement structure materially moves the per-rack number.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"e6\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market: contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": [\"new-memory-prices\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 81, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}], \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\", \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"E1\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, with the GPUs not driving the increase; the bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"E2\", \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a roughly threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5-10% on GB200 to 25-30% on VR200 while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"E3\", \"summary\": \"First shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"E4\", \"summary\": \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, reflecting the same constrained DRAM/NAND/HBM supply against AI-driven demand that is raising component costs tied to the AI buildout.\", \"links\": []}]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"memdiag-packet-claims-3.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps({\\\"n\\\":x[\\\"n\\\"],\\\"claims\\\":[c[\\\"claim_id\\\"] for c in x[\\\"claims\\\"]],\\\"usable\\\":x[\\\"answer_usable\\\"],\\\"evidence\\\":[[e[\\\"id\\\"],e[\\\"summary\\\"]] for e in x[\\\"kept_evidence\\\"]]})) for x in p if 63<=x[\\\"n\\\"]<=73]' \"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"memdiag-packet-claims-3.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps({\\\"n\\\":x[\\\"n\\\"],\\\"claims\\\":[c[\\\"claim_id\\\"] for c in x[\\\"claims\\\"]],\\\"usable\\\":x[\\\"answer_usable\\\"],\\\"evidence\\\":[[e[\\\"id\\\"],e[\\\"summary\\\"]] for e in x[\\\"kept_evidence\\\"]]})) for x in p if 63<=x[\\\"n\\\"]<=73]' \"","aggregated_output":"{\"n\": 63, \"claims\": [\"M1\", \"M2\", \"M3\"], \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [[\"ev-1\", \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and the bank estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; the increase is attributed to components other than the GPU.\"], [\"ev-2\", \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5\\u201310 percent on the GB200 to 25\\u201330 percent on the VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"], [\"ev-3\", \"NVIDIA's Vera Rubin systems are scheduled to make first shipments in the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, according to the article; the schedule is reported without naming who set it.\"], [\"ev-4\", \"The article reports that contract DDR5 memory pricing has climbed sharply as constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory meets AI-driven demand, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"], [\"ev-5\", \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the price of an NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack down to around $6.7 million from roughly $8 million, indicating that procurement structure materially moves the per-rack number.\"]]}\n{\"n\": 64, \"claims\": [\"M1\", \"M2\", \"M3\"], \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [[\"e1\", \"Morgan Stanley Research's bill-of-materials analysis estimates NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. Memory content is put at about $2 million per rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage virtually absent from earlier systems. Memory's share of the bill of materials rises to 25-30% on the VR200 from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%.\"], [\"e2\", \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, and says the GPUs themselves are not the driver of the rack price increase.\"], [\"e3\", \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, according to the schedule reported in Morgan Stanley Research's bill-of-materials analysis.\"], [\"e4\", \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that with memory representing a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, memory availability and lead times warrant the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\"], [\"e5\", \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, showing that procurement structure can move the final rack price by more than $1 million per rack.\"]]}\n{\"n\": 65, \"claims\": [\"M1\", \"M2\", \"M3\"], \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [[\"e1\", \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, according to the bill-of-materials analysis cited by MarketScale.\"], [\"e2\", \"Morgan Stanley estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when those chips are sold in volume.\"], [\"e3\", \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage; memory's share of the bill of materials rises from 5-10% on GB200 to 25-30% on VR200 while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%.\"], [\"e4\", \"In the analysis cited by MarketScale, first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\"], [\"e5\", \"The article reports that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass the higher memory costs into upcoming products, tying the AI buildout to the same memory supply squeeze as consumer devices.\"], [\"e6\", \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, meaning procurement structure can move final pricing by more than $1 million per rack.\"]]}\n{\"n\": 66, \"claims\": [\"M1\", \"M2\", \"M3\"], \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [[\"ev-1\", \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. In the bank's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content is about $2 million per rack (a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a roughly threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage), memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5\\u201310% on the GB200 to 25\\u201330% on the VR200, and the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\"], [\"ev-2\", \"According to the Morgan Stanley Research analysis reported by MarketScale, first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\"], [\"ev-3\", \"The item reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is the force behind the higher Vera Rubin rack price and is raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"], [\"ev-4\", \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules for NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the roughly $7.8 million estimated purchase price, indicating procurement structure materially moves the final number.\"]]}\n{\"n\": 67, \"claims\": [\"M1\"], \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [[\"e1\", \"Morgan Stanley Research's bill-of-materials analysis estimates Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. Memory content per rack is put at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, on a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage; as a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5\\u201310% on GB200 to 25\\u201330% on VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\"], [\"e2\", \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"], [\"e3\", \"Morgan Stanley Research's breakdown has Nvidia's first Vera Rubin shipments scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\"], [\"e4\", \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is pushing up component costs, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"], [\"e5\", \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, and that procurement structure can move final pricing by $1+ million per rack.\"]]}\n{\"n\": 68, \"claims\": [\"M1\", \"M2\", \"M3\"], \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [[\"e1\", \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation; the bank estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU in volume, and notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\"], [\"e2\", \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content in NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million per rack, a 435% increase over the prior generation, driven by a threefold rise in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage; memory's share of the bill of materials rose to 25\\u201330% on VR200 from 5\\u201310% on GB200, while the GPU share fell from about 63% to 51%.\"], [\"e3\", \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\"], [\"e4\", \"The item reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs market-wide, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that with memory representing a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, availability and lead times need the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\"]]}\n{\"n\": 69, \"claims\": [\"M1\"], \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [[\"e1\", \"Morgan Stanley Research, reported by MarketScale, estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, with Nvidia charging about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. The bank puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND; memory's share of the bill of materials rises to 25-30% on the VR200 from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%, and a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\"], [\"e2\", \"MarketScale reports that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\"], [\"e3\", \"MarketScale reports that contract DDR5 memory pricing has climbed sharply, that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that the AI buildout and consumer-device memory costs are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\"]]}\n{\"n\": 70, \"claims\": [\"M1\", \"M2\", \"M3\"], \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [[\"ev-1\", \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. Memory is the driver of the increase: about $2 million of memory content per rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND. Memory rises from 5-10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25-30% on VR200, while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%. Reported by MarketScale from Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis.\"], [\"ev-2\", \"Morgan Stanley estimates that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, with the article stating the GPUs themselves are not the driver of the rack price increase.\"], [\"ev-3\", \"The item reports that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter; no direct attribution for the schedule is given in the article.\"], [\"ev-4\", \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, and the article states procurement structure can move final rack pricing by more than $1 million per rack.\"], [\"ev-5\", \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"]]}\n{\"n\": 71, \"claims\": [\"M1\"], \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [[\"ev-1\", \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates, in a bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"], [\"ev-2\", \"According to the Morgan Stanley Research analysis reported by MarketScale, first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin (VR200) racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter; the item gives no Nvidia confirmation of the schedule.\"], [\"ev-3\", \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a roughly threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory's share of the bill of materials rises to 25\\u201330% on VR200 from 5\\u201310% on GB200, while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%.\"], [\"ev-4\", \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin VR200 rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus its roughly $7.8 million estimate, showing that procurement structure materially moves the per-rack figure.\"], [\"ev-5\", \"MarketScale reports, citing constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand as the force behind rising AI rack prices, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"]]}\n{\"n\": 72, \"claims\": [\"M1\", \"M2\", \"M3\"], \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [[\"ev1\", \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, with NVIDIA charging about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\"], [\"ev2\", \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory rose from 5 to 10 percent of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"], [\"ev3\", \"Morgan Stanley's analysis states NVIDIA's Vera Rubin first shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\"], [\"ev4\", \"The article reports that supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory is constrained against AI-driven demand, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, tying the AI buildout and laptop memory prices to the same supply squeeze.\"]]}\n{\"n\": 73, \"claims\": [\"M1\", \"M2\", \"M3\"], \"usable\": true, \"evidence\": [[\"ev-rack-price\", \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"], [\"ev-memory-bom\", \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; as a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5-10 percent on the GB200 to 25-30 percent on the VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"], [\"ev-ship-schedule\", \"According to the Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter of 2026.\"], [\"ev-memory-supply-price\", \"The item reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass the higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"], [\"ev-self-sourcing\", \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the price of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack down to around $6.7 million, meaning procurement structure can move final rack pricing by more than $1 million per rack.\"]]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
